From 7a62d7fa667fb599264cad6bb421196812286310 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MasherDon <62326358+MasherDon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 19:30:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/91] moe-stream : stage the next calls' experts into VRAM
 (oracle), keep-first victims, NVTX, read counters

- Staging (A4b; LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_VRAM_AHEAD=V, oracle only for now): the remap of call c, after
  queueing its own demand, reserves slots for the experts of calls c+1..c+V in their layers and queues
  the loads on q_stage (after demand, before speculation; the demand-only workers take it first, the
  speculating ones only when there is nothing to speculate). The uploads then run while the GPU
  computes instead of after the next router. Staged experts still count as misses, and the stats say
  how many were ready when their call came. Exact: same text as the trace. Oracle 200 calls ahead,
  17 I/O threads: 10.2 -> 13.3 tok/s (medians); copies overlapping compute 0.00 -> 10.5 ms per token.
- keep_first (on; LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_KEEP_FIRST=0 for A/B): the remap protects every slot its call reads
  before picking a victim. Before, a miss early in the list could evict an expert that a later entry of
  the same call needed, which was then read straight back. Misses 59614 -> 58220 on the same text.
- LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_SPEC_VRAM=1 (off): speculate experts that are in VRAM now as well. Measured worse
  (10.5 vs 13.2 tok/s): the extra reads push useful slabs out of the host tier.
- NVTX ranges, free without a profiler (header-only NVTX3 from the CUDA toolkit, found by CMake):
  remap / stall / speculate / stage / victim on the graph thread, l2 / read / upload.wait / upload /
  spec on the I/O threads, payload = layer.
- Drive-read counters: time inside the positional read per path, a coarse histogram and the average
  number of reads in flight, printed with the stats.

SiegeLadder runs.md 4.21.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
